using System;
using System.Xml;
using System.Xml.XPath;

public class XPathHelper
{
    public T GetElementWithAttributes<T>(XmlDocument xmlDoc, string xpath, string namespaceUri) where T : new()
    {
        var node = xmlDoc.SelectSingleNode($"/{namespaceUri}:{xpath}", xmlDoc.DocumentElement.NamespaceManager);

        if (node == null)
            throw new XPathException("No matching element found.");

        if (node.HasAttributes && node.ParentNode.ChildNodes.Count(c => c.Name == node.Name) > 1)
            throw new XPathException("Multiple elements with the given name were found.");

        var result = Activator.CreateInstance<T>();

        CopyProperties(node, result);

        return result;
    }

    private void CopyProperties<T>(XmlNode node, T instance)
    {
        var properties = typeof(T).GetProperties();

        foreach (var property in properties)
        {
            var xmlAttribute = node.Attributes[property.Name];

            if (xmlAttribute != null)
                property.SetValue(instance, XmlConvert.ToString(xmlAttribute.Value));
        }
    }
}
```

In the example above, I provided a `XPathHelper` class that includes two functions: `GetElementWithAttributes` and `CopyProperties`. The `GetElementWithAttributes` function takes an XML document, XPath query, and namespace URI as input. It uses the XPath expression with the given namespace to find the first matching element with attributes, handles edge cases such as missing elements or multiple matches, and returns a new instance of the generic type `T` with its properties set to the attribute values from the XML node.

The `CopyProperties` function is used internally in `GetElementWithAttributes` to copy the attribute values to the created instance of `T`. This function utilizes reflection to get the properties of the generic type and loops through them to find and assign attribute values from the XmlNode to the corresponding property.']

[' Here is a Java method using `Executors` for managing threads and `ForkJoinPool` for efficient processing. I have used `CompletableFuture` to handle exceptions and manage resources. The method limits the number of concurrent threads using a fixed thread pool with a given capacity.

```java
import java.math.BigInteger;
import java.nio.charset.StandardCharsets;
import java.security.MessageDigest;
import java.util.ArrayList;
import java.util.List;
import java.util.concurrent.*;
import static java.util.stream.Collectors.toList;

public class MD5Hasher {
    private static final int THREAD_CAPACITY = 10;

    public static List<String> processStringsConcurrently(List<String> inputStrings) {
        ExecutorService executor = Executors.newFixedThreadPool(THREAD_CAPACITY);
        List<CompletableFuture<String>> futures = new ArrayList<>();

        for (String input : inputStrings) {
            final String str = input;
            CompletableFuture<String> future = CompletableFuture.supplyAsync(() -> md5Hash(str), executor);
            futures.add(future);
        }

        List<String> results = new ArrayList<>();
        for (CompletableFuture<String> future : futures) {
            try {
                String result = future.get();
                results.add(result);
            } catch (ExecutionException | InterruptedException | CancellationException e) {
                // Handle exceptions as needed
            }
        }
        executor.shutdown();

        return results;
    }

    private static String md5Hash(String input) {
        try {
            MessageDigest digest = MessageDigest.getInstance("MD5");
            byte[] hash = digest.digest(input.getBytes(StandardCharsets.UTF_8));
            BigInteger bigInt = new BigInteger(1, hash);
            String hashtext = bigInt.toString(16);

            while (hashtext.length() < 32) {
                hashtext = "0" + hashtext;
            }
            return hashtext;
        } catch (Exception e) {
            throw new RuntimeException(e);
        }
    }
}
```']

