[' Here is a simplified C++ example using `std::thread`, `std::mutex`, `std::unique_ptr`, `std::vector` and `boost::asio` library for I/O operations. This code does not include error handling, logging, metrics collection, external dependency integration, dynamic thread adjustment based on system load, or graceful shutdown mechanisms, as those would require a more extensive design and implementation.

```cpp
#include <iostream>
#include <vector>
#include <memory>
#include <boost/asio.hpp>
#include <mutex>
#include <thread>
#include <fstream>
#include <sstream>

struct FileDescriptorManager {
    std::unique_ptr<char[]> buffer;
    std::vector<std::ifstream> fileDescriptors;
    size_t maxThreads = 4;
    int currentThreadCount = 0;
    std::mutex mutex;
    bool cancelRequested = false;
};

void readFile(FileDescriptorManager& manager, size_t index) {
    unique_lock<mutex> lock(manager.mutex);
    if (!manager.fileDescriptors[index].is_open()) return;
    if (manager.buffer.get() == nullptr || manager.cancelRequested) {
        manager.buffer.reset();
        return;
    }

    stringstream bufferStream;
    string line;
    while (getline(manager.fileDescriptors[index], line)) {
        bufferStream << line << "\n";
    }

    lock.unlock();
    if (bufferStream.str().size() > 0) {
        manager.buffer.get() += bufferStream.str();
    }
}

std::unique_ptr<char[]> readMultipleFilesConcurrently(FileDescriptorManager& manager, const std::vector<const char*>& filePaths) {
    for (auto path : filePaths) manager.fileDescriptors.emplace_back(path);

    if (!manager.buffer) manager.buffer = std::make_unique<char[]>(1);

    for (size_t i = 0; i < manager.maxThreads && i < manager.fileDescriptors.size(); ++i) {
        std::thread(readFile, std::ref(manager), i).detach();
        ++manager.currentThreadCount;
    }

    while (manager.currentThreadCount > 0) usleep(100000); // Sleep for 100ms to allow threads to finish.

    if (manager.buffer->size() == 0) manager.buffer.reset();
    for (auto& file : manager.fileDescriptors) file.close();
    manager.currentThreadCount = 0;

    return manager.buffer;
}
```

This code creates a `FileDescriptorManager` structure, which manages the buffer and file descriptors. The `readFile` function is used by threads to read data from individual files and accumulate it in the shared buffer. The `readMultipleFilesConcurrently` function dynamically creates a specified number of threads (up to 4) to perform concurrent I/O operations on the provided file paths. It returns the concatenated file contents or null if there is insufficient memory. The 'next' pointer of the last file descriptor in the list is maintained by the shared buffer, and thread safety is ensured using a mutex for critical sections.']

