For the file hashing utility implementation, you can use a solution that combines ForkJoinPool for parallel processing, ByteBuffer for memory efficiency, and MessageDigest for calculating MD5 hashes. Here's an example of how to implement this:

```java
public String hashFile(Path filePath, boolean parallelize) throws InterruptedException, ExecutionException {
    MessageDigest md = MessageDigest.getInstance("MD5");
    long chunkSize = 1 << 20; // 1MB chunks
    ByteBuffer buffer = ByteBuffer.allocateDirect((int)filePath.toFile().length());

    FileInputStream fis = null;
    try {
        fis = new FileInputStream(filePath.toFile());
        byte[] data = new byte[chunkSize];
        int readBytes;
        if (parallelize) {
            ForkJoinPool pool = new ForkJoinPool();
            ForkJoinTask<Void> task = new HashFileTask(fis, buffer, md, chunkSize);
            pool.invokeAll(Iterables.concat(Arrays.asList(EnhancedFuture.traverse(task)), Lists.newArrayList(task)));
        } else {
            while ((readBytes = fis.read(data)) != -1) {
                buffer.position(buffer.position() + readBytes);
                buffer.put(data, 0, readBytes);
                buffer.flip();
                md.update(buffer);
                buffer.clear();
            }
        }
    } finally {
        if (fis != null) fis.close();
    }

    byte[] digest = md.digest();
    StringBuilder hexString = new StringBuilder();
    for (byte b : digest) {
        hexString.append(String.format("%02x", b));
    }
    return hexString.toString();
}
